==Translingual==

===Etymology===
From {{der|mul|ar|ا|tr=-|pos=[[alif]]}}. The Cyrillic form was originally the Roman numeral [[Ⅰ]] that was furnished on most Russian manual typewriters. The Roman I key did double duty for the Hindu-Arabic digit [[1]].

===Letter===
{{mul-letter}}

# {{Cyrl-def|mul|letter||palochka}}

====Usage notes====
Often the capital Roman numeral {{m|mul|Ⅰ}} or the Hindu-Arabic digit {{m|mul|1}} is used instead of the Unicode palochka. For some languages, such as [[Chechen]], national keyboards do not include a key specifically for a palochka, and so the Roman numeral or the digit is in standard use.

